===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To perform as a [[human blockhead]].
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=2007|title=Vaudeville old & new: an encyclopedia of variety performances in America |author=Frank Cullen, ‎Florence Hackman, & ‎Donald McNeilly   |page= |ISBN=0415938538 |passage=Like an old-time sideshow, the acts included lying on a bed of nails, '''blockheading''' and, of course, fire-eating. }}
# {{lb|en|rare}} To behave in a [[stupid]] manner.
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1804|title=The Spirit of the Public Journals |author=  |page= |ISBN= |passage=Two years' blockading made fair Malta ours: A noble struggle! -- yet 't is thought (at Rading) That Britain's island shews superior pow'rs, To bear, and to survive, two years '''blockheading''' !   }}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1856|title=The works of John Adams, second President of the United States |author=John Adams  |page= |ISBN=1623764637 |passage=I have '''blockheading''' and boxing enough at Master Lovell's, I won't have it repeated here; and in a great passion, I threw the Virgil at his head, hit him in the face, and bruised his lip, and ran away. }}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1958|title=The Ethical Outlook: A Journal of the American Ethical Union |author=  |page= |ISBN= |passage=The operation of '''blockheading''' is much the same as that of beheading, which Charles I underwent. In both the head is severed from the body. The body goes around without benefit of head.  The victim becomes a Headless Horseman, or that more common figure a Headless Pedestrian. In our day the operation is often done not on an executioner's block but on Paradise Avenue in Suburbia Manor. }}

